  "sentences_over_45": [
   {
    "words": 54,
    "section": "Introduction",
    "subsection": null,
    "subsubsection": null,
    "text": "Application records joined with bureau histories, previous-loan behaviour and transaction aggregates at several depths and time windows yield hundreds to thousands of candidate predictors observed on hundreds of thousands to millions of decisions, many of the predictors redundant, some unstable over time and a few unavailable at the moment a lending decision is taken ."
   },
   {
    "words": 61,
    "section": "Introduction",
    "subsection": null,
    "subsubsection": null,
    "text": "Supervised selectors are refitted in every validation fold and at every model refresh, and their cost grows with the number of decisions and, for redundancy-aware selectors, with the number of candidates over which redundancy is computed: on the first development fold of Stability 2024, one mRMR pass took 522 seconds over the 1,959-feature universe and 21 seconds over a 100-feature pool."
   },
   {
    "words": 92,
    "section": "Introduction",
    "subsection": null,
    "subsubsection": null,
    "text": "First, a held-out comparison, temporal for LendingClub and Stability 2024 and recency-shifted for Home Credit, of classical, model-based and LLM pre-screened selection, whose semantic stage reads no data rows, across three credit portfolios of up to 1,959 engineered features and 1.5 million decisions (1,526,659) and two backbones; the six paired HO comparisons between the methods with the highest observed HO AUC in each family are reported as effect sizes with paired bootstrap intervals, and repeated under two further leader-choice rules, one of which uses no held-out information to choose the compared methods."
   },
   {
    "words": 73,
    "section": "Introduction",
    "subsection": null,
    "subsubsection": null,
    "text": "Section  REF reviews related work, Section  REF gives background, Sections  REF and REF specify the methods and the experimental setup, and Section  REF reports the results in the order of the questions a reader is likely to ask: the headline comparison (Section  REF ), its robustness (Section  REF ), what drives it (Section  REF ), stability, drift and secondary metrics (Section  REF ) and scalability and cost (Section  REF ); Section  REF discusses them and Section  REF concludes."
   }
  ],
